1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,936
Six months after the stolen phone, a letter comes to the bank.

2
00:00:03,986 --> 00:00:09,768
Aziz writes that on that Friday night, before the block, someone approved a login on a laptop, and it was not him.

3
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:11,640
He wants to know what happened.

4
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:18,264
The bank wants to know too, and it also wants to show that its answer is the truth, not a story written afterwards.

5
00:00:18,314 --> 00:00:23,568
How does a platform keep a record that even its own administrators cannot rewrite?

6
00:00:23,618 --> 00:00:30,984
In Lesson 35, every message the platform sent left through the outbox, with its template version in an audit event.

7
00:00:31,034 --> 00:00:40,080
This lesson follows those audit events: what goes into one, why nobody can change it, who reads the stream, and how long each record stays.

8
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:41,952
Start with one event.

9
00:00:42,002 --> 00:00:50,184
Each one has the event type, the app, the time to the millisecond, and the actor: a customer reference, a staff identity, or the system.

10
00:00:50,234 --> 00:00:56,040
It has the device, the network address, and the registration, session or case it belongs to.

11
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:05,208
It has the result and a reason key, the MyID references when there are any, and, for a read of identity data, the reader and the purpose.

12
00:01:05,258 --> 00:01:17,280
The list of event types is long on purpose: registration, proofing, linking, devices, logins, administration, notifications, apps, sessions and resets.

13
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:19,152
Now, what an event never has.

14
00:01:19,202 --> 00:01:26,328
No document images, no full document numbers, no full personal identification number, no addresses.

15
00:01:26,378 --> 00:01:33,456
No codes, no passwords, no result codes, no reusable identifiers, no access grants.

16
00:01:33,506 --> 00:01:41,616
The identifiers are the platform's own references, and the personal identification number shows only its first two and last two digits.

17
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:44,568
So the trail can travel without becoming a leak.

18
00:01:44,618 --> 00:01:46,800
Second: nobody can change it.

19
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:49,104
The platform only adds events.

20
00:01:49,154 --> 00:01:55,800
No operation of the system, and no administrator action, can change or delete an event before its retention ends.

21
00:01:55,850 --> 00:02:01,560
The database role of the application simply has no right to update or delete that table.

22
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:07,416
But a person with direct access to the database could still try, so the trail protects itself with a chain.

23
00:02:07,466 --> 00:02:15,912
Each event gets an HMAC: a keyed fingerprint of its sequence number, the fingerprint of the event before it, and the event itself.

24
00:02:15,962 --> 00:02:19,848
The key lives in Vault, as the audit key of the installation.

25
00:02:19,898 --> 00:02:26,256
Change one old event, and its fingerprint no longer matches, and neither does any fingerprint after it.

26
00:02:26,306 --> 00:02:31,152
Every day a verifier walks the chain and raises an alert at the first break.

27
00:02:31,202 --> 00:02:37,344
I think this is what makes a trail worth keeping: we do not ask the bank to trust us, we let it check us.

28
00:02:37,394 --> 00:02:39,216
Third: who reads it.

29
00:02:39,266 --> 00:02:46,752
Each event goes to the security monitoring of the bank as a continuous feed, through a Kafka topic, within seconds.

30
00:02:46,802 --> 00:02:52,824
If the feed is down, the platform keeps the events and sends them later, in the correct order.

31
00:02:52,874 --> 00:03:02,952
A second stream goes to the bank's antifraud system: risk events, such as a login, a block or a password change, with references and no personal data.

32
00:03:03,002 --> 00:03:09,480
And Compliance can export events by period, by customer and by event type, in a format that a machine can read.

33
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:11,352
Back to Aziz's letter.

34
00:03:11,402 --> 00:03:14,280
Compliance exports his events for that Friday.

35
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:20,832
The trail shows a login on the marketplace web, a correct password, and an approval request to his trusted phone.

36
00:03:20,882 --> 00:03:27,168
It shows that the approval came from that phone, twenty minutes after the theft and half an hour before the block.

37
00:03:27,218 --> 00:03:30,456
It shows the block, and later the revoke of that same phone.

38
00:03:30,506 --> 00:03:37,416
So the approval came from the stolen phone, and the chain verifies, so nobody wrote these lines after the letter arrived.

39
00:03:37,466 --> 00:03:40,296
Fourth: how long each record lives.

40
00:03:40,346 --> 00:03:45,024
Every kind of record has its own clock, and the bank configures the long ones.

41
00:03:45,074 --> 00:03:49,992
A one-time code lives until it is used or expires, and then it is deleted.

42
00:03:50,042 --> 00:03:57,024
A registration that never reached level one is deleted after the registration-expiry period, but its audit events stay.

43
00:03:57,074 --> 00:04:04,008
An earlier version of a verified identity stays for the identity retention, counted from the day it was replaced.

44
00:04:04,058 --> 00:04:11,808
The customer and the current identity stay for the life of the customer, then for the identity retention after closure.

45
00:04:11,858 --> 00:04:19,176
Audit events stay for the audit retention that the bank's regulation asks for, and then they leave in groups of full days.

46
00:04:19,226 --> 00:04:23,328
Policy and text versions stay for the life of the installation.

47
00:04:23,378 --> 00:04:29,832
Deletion removes a record from the active store and, within the backup rotation, from every backup.

48
00:04:29,882 --> 00:04:35,088
Nothing is deleted early, and each month a deletion report shows that the rule held.

49
00:04:35,138 --> 00:04:39,528
To recap: one event for each step, with no secret inside it.

50
00:04:39,578 --> 00:04:46,944
An HMAC chain that the bank can check, a feed to its monitoring and its antifraud system, and a clock for every record.

51
00:04:46,994 --> 00:04:51,384
But someone had to export that Friday, and someone else pressed block.

52
00:04:51,434 --> 00:04:55,512
Who are these people, what may each of them do, and who checks them?

53
00:04:55,562 --> 00:04:57,864
Next: the administrator panel.
